{"id":"project-LICHEN-worker6-aen8","title":"[review] project-LICHEN-worker6-kkj5: transcript_4 credential asymmetry — initiator vs responder use different credential formats","description":"In `EdhocInitiator::prepare_message_3` (line 949), TH_4 uses `peer.credential` — the full credential received from the responder in EDHOC message_2.

```rust
self.state.th_4 = transcript_4(&self.state.th_3, &plaintext_3, peer.credential)?;
```

In `EdhocResponder::process_message_3` (lines 1416-1419), TH_4 uses a freshly-encoded COSE_Key:

```rust
let mut credential_r = heapless::Vec::<u8, 80>::new();
encode_credential(&mut credential_r, self.pubkey.as_bytes())?;
self.state.th_4 = transcript_4(&self.state.th_3, &pending.plaintext, &credential_r)?;
```

These will produce different TH_4 values unless the credential format transmitted in message_2 happens to be a COSE_Key (rather than CWT, X.509, or another format). The responder should use its own credential (as transmitted), not a reconstructed COSE_Key from the raw public key.

**Severity**: P0 — cryptographic mismatch would prevent successful EDHOC handshake completion for many credential types.

**Note**: This is related to the CBOR `0xa2`/`0xa3` bug; even after fixing that, the credential format mismatch remains.","status":"in_progress","priority":0,"issue_type":"bug","assignee":"AI Agent","owner":"agent@lichen-project.dev","created_at":"2026-07-24T19:05:47.805783284Z","created_by":"AI Agent","updated_at":"2026-07-24T19:18:23.352099169Z","started_at":"2026-07-24T19:18:23.352099169Z","lease_expires_at":"2026-07-24T19:23:23.352099169Z","heartbeat_at":"2026-07-24T19:18:23.352099169Z"}

